    <item>
      <title>Gunther von Hagens, Creator of Polarizing ‘Body Worlds’ Exhibits, Dies at 81</title>
      <link>https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/28/science/gunther-von-hagens-dead.html</link>
      <guid isPermaLink="true">https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/28/science/gunther-von-hagens-dead.html</guid>
      <atom:link href="https://www.nytimes.com/2026/07/28/science/gunther-von-hagens-dead.html" rel="standout"></atom:link>
      <description>A German scientist, he developed a process for turning corpses into posable figures. His shows drew millions of visitors, and many detractors.</description>
      <dc:creator>Clay Risen</dc:creator>
      <pubDate>Tue, 28 Jul 2026 21:57:56 +0000</pubDate>
      <category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/des">Cadavers</category>
      <category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/des">Deaths (Obituaries)</category>
      <category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/des">Anatomy and Physiology</category>
      <category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_ttl">Body Worlds (Exhibit)</category>
      <category domain="http://www.nytimes.com/namespaces/keywords/nyt_per">Gunther von Hagens (1945-2026)</category>
      <media:content height="1800" medium="image" url="https://static01.nyt.com/images/2026/07/27/multimedia/00vonhagens-cjpk/00vonhagens-cjpk-mediumSquareAt3X.jpg" width="1800"></media:content>
      <media:credit>Sion Touhig/Getty Images</media:credit>
      <media:description>Gunther von Hagens in 2002 with a flayed, bisected human specimen used in one of his “Body Worlds” exhibitions. In the late 1970s, he developed plastination, the method of making corpses into malleable figures.</media:description>
    </item>
